<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shendi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shendi is a town on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, near the ruins of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>. In the 18th and early 19th centuries it was one of the largest markets of the Nile valley, a meeting point of caravans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the interior, described by the traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a> in 1814. It was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaalin">Ja'alin</a> chiefs, and in 1822 the Egyptian prince Ismail was killed there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shendi.jpg" alt="Shendi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: محسن الفكي · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%87%D8%B1%D9%85_%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A_-_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%AC%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%88%D9%8A%D8%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
